jt-pve-storage-netapp (0.2.21-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * Orphan-Cleanup N+1 REST Storm Fix (ONTAP mgmt-gateway load)
  * Requirements: Proxmox VE 9.1 or later
  * Bug fix (production incident, customer 2026-06-16, FAS on ONTAP
    9.15.1P19; a sibling ASA on 9.14.1 with the same plugin was fine):
    - After a firmware upgrade, the FAS management REST became slow
      (~4s/request) and intermittently refused connections; pvestatd
      flapped the storage 'inactive'. Disabling the storage cluster-wide
      drained it slowly (19s -> 14s -> 12s ...), proving the load came
      from PVE polling, not a FAS outage. cluster show was healthy.
    - Root cause: _cleanup_orphaned_devices() (run in the status()
      background cleanup on EVERY ~10s poll on EVERY cluster node) called
      lun_get_wwid($lun->{name}) once PER LUN to build its alive-set --
      an N+1 REST storm (lun_get_serial -> lun_get -> GET per LUN). An SVM
      with 75 LUNs x N nodes generated ~75*N REST calls every 10s, enough
      to overwhelm ONTAP's management gateway (mgwd) into congestion
      collapse -- especially on 9.15.1P19, whose mgwd is far more load-
      sensitive than the ASA's 9.14.1 under the identical plugin load.
      Not an explicit rate limit (no HTTP 429) -- mgwd capacity exhaustion.
    - Fix: lun_list() already returns serial_number for every LUN in ONE
      paginated call, so compute the WWID locally via serial_to_wwid()
      (pure, no REST) instead of the per-LUN call. ~75 calls/poll -> 0
      extra; the computed alive-set is byte-identical (verified on real
      ONTAP). Behaviour is otherwise unchanged.
  * Testing: docs/TESTING.md Section 30 added. New real-ONTAP test
    (tests/cleanup_load.pl) 6/6: alloc 3 LUNs, instrument the API, assert
    ZERO per-LUN lun_get calls during cleanup AND every WWID still in the
    alive-set, then verify 0 leftover. Full regression: sim_functional
    13/13, unit reaper 20/20 + status-timeout 13/13 + activate-budget 8/8,
    make test all modules OK. The other per-poll calls were audited:
    get_managed_capacity early-returns on the aggregate (1 call, no N+1);
    _check_aggregate_capacity (1h cooldown) and _check_lif_redundancy
    (24h cooldown) already throttle their queries.

