jt-pve-storage-netapp (0.2.19-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * pvestatd Isolation + Stale-Path Reaper + Connection Reuse Release
  * Requirements: Proxmox VE 9.1 or later
  * Fix 1 -- stale SCSI-path reaper (LUN-ID reuse on a non-teardown node):
    - Customer node could not build the multipath map for a live LUN
      ("device-mapper: error getting device (-EBUSY)"); "multipath -ll"
      showed nothing while other nodes were fine. Per-node igroup mode
      maps every LUN to ALL nodes; a node that never ran the LUN's
      free_image() keeps stale sd paths; ONTAP reuses the freed SCSI
      LUN-ID for a new LUN; the stale sd (now reports no WWID) shadows
      the reused LUN-ID and device-mapper cannot load the new map.
    - The v0.2.18 teardown sweep matches by WWID, so it cannot catch
      these (they advertise no matchable WWID).
    - Fix: new Multipath::list_netapp_scsi_paths() (raw-sd topology
      enumeration) + _reap_stale_scsi_paths() as a third pass in
      _cleanup_orphaned_devices(). Removes an sd ONLY when: vendor is
      NETAPP, it has no holders and is not mounted, AND either it is an
      orphan of a deleted LUN this storage tracked, OR a sibling sd at
      the same iSCSI target IQN + same LUN-ID reports a live (alive-set)
      WWID while this one differs/blank (reused LUN-ID). 300s grace;
      anything indeterminate is left alone. Reap then self-heals with
      rescan + multipath reload.
  * Fix 2 -- pvestatd timeout isolation (degraded ONTAP no longer wedges
    PVE or sibling storages):
    - During a customer's ONTAP upgrade, one controller's mgmt REST
      read-timed-out; activate_storage/status stacked several 15s x 2
      retry calls into "status update time (189s)", and pvestatd's
      sequential storage loop dragged a sibling storage on the same node
      into 'inactive'.
    - Fix: new "ontap-status-timeout" option (default 5s). The pvestatd
      health path (activate_storage/status foreground) uses a short
      timeout, single-attempt API client; the next ~10s poll is the
      retry. Data path (alloc/free/clone) keeps the resilient client.
      Measured fast-fail: 5.0s vs 32.0s.
  * Fix 3 -- HTTP keep-alive (do not overwhelm ONTAP's mgmt gateway):
    - A request storm (no connection reuse + per-request basic auth,
      multiplied across cluster nodes and retries) overwhelmed ONTAP's
      mgmt gateway into sustained read timeouts (proven: 15s/req while
      hammered, 0.5s the instant polling stopped). LWP now uses
      keep_alive => 1 so REST calls reuse one TCP+TLS connection instead
      of a new handshake + re-auth per call.
  * Testing: docs/TESTING.md Section 28 added. Simulator functional test
    (real ONTAP + real host devices) 13/13: full alloc/activate/free
    lifecycle with host-side device assertions, stale-sd reaper
    no-false-positive on a healthy live device, degraded fast-fail
    5.0s vs 32.0s. Unit tests: reaper decision logic 20/20, status-path
    client 13/13. ONTAP + host both verified 0 leftover.

