jt-pve-storage-netapp (0.2.18-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * Stale SCSI Path Sweep on Cleanup Release
  * Requirements: Proxmox VE 9.1 or later
  * Hardening (independent issue from v0.2.17 incident log):
    - Kernel logged "LUN assignments on this target have changed. The
      Linux SCSI layer does not automatically remap LUN assignments."
      ONTAP auto-assigns SCSI LUN-IDs on lun_map and reuses a freed
      LUN-ID for a different LUN after unmap. If a stale host sd device
      is still bound to that H:C:T:L, the new LUN is not usable on that
      path and the kernel refuses to remap.
    - Root gap: cleanup_lun_devices() removed only the paths CURRENTLY
      in the multipath map, and was a no-op entirely when the map was
      already gone -- leaving orphaned single sd paths that later
      collide with reused LUN-IDs.
    - Fix: new Multipath::get_scsi_paths_for_wwid() enumerates ALL
      NETAPP sd paths for a WWID (incl. paths no longer in the map);
      cleanup_lun_devices() Step 8 sweeps them even when the map is
      gone. Vendor-gated to NETAPP, WWID-matched, and bounded by a
      wall-clock budget (default 30s) so a host with hundreds of sd
      devices and failing paths cannot stall teardown (v0.2.12 lesson:
      per-read timeouts do not bound cumulative time).
  * Testing: docs/TESTING.md Section 27 added (helper matching, Step 8
    orphan sweep, static guards). Verified on simulator pc-pve1:
    get_scsi_paths_for_wwid matches real paths and rejects bogus WWIDs;
    Step 8 sweeps sd paths orphaned by a bare multipath -f.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Thu, 29 May 2026 20:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-netapp (0.2.17-1) stable; urgency=high

  * Orphan Reaper Path-Health Gate + LUN List Pagination Release
  * Requirements: Proxmox VE 9.1 or later
  * Bug Fix (production incident, customer report 2026-05, node pve15):
    - Hot-adding a disk to a RUNNING VM could cause immediate I/O
      errors on that brand-new disk. _cleanup_orphaned_devices()
      built its "alive set" from a single lun_list() snapshot and
      reaped any tracked WWID missing from it -- but a freshly-created
      LUN can be absent from that bulk query for a window (ONTAP
      read-after-write / propagation lag, same class as v0.2.9 ASA).
      The reaper then tore down the live multipath device with NO
      check of path health. Because the VM was running, QEMU held the
      device open, so multipath -f failed and the dmsetup remove
      --force fallback ripped the map out from under QEMU -> I/O error.
      Power-cycling the VM "fixed" it (multipath re-acquired on next
      activate, by which time lun_list no longer lagged).
    - Fix 1 (path-health gate): new Multipath::multipath_path_health()
      -- the reaper NEVER removes a device that still has an active
      path (or whose state is indeterminate). A genuine orphan has ALL
      paths failed. Applied to both the first-pass reap and the
      second-pass "untracked stale" operator warning.
    - Fix 2 (grace period): a WWID tracked within the last 300s is not
      reaped, covering the read-after-write window. Reuses the existing
      first-tracked timestamp; no new state file.
    - Fix 3 (LUN list pagination): API::lun_list() now follows ONTAP
      REST _links.next so an SVM with 1000+ LUNs never silently
      truncates the alive set. Same pagination applied to volume_list,
      volume_get_clone_children, igroup_list, snapshot_list.
  * Testing: docs/TESTING.md Section 26 added (path-health logic,
    pagination, static guards, RUNNING-VM functional reproduce with
    host-side assertions, grace-period guard). Full simulator test
    PASS incl. both reaper defenses, real-orphan still reaped, and
    full disk lifecycle with clean host-side teardown.

